---
import PrimerChat from "../PrimerChat.astro";
import { aiCardId, aiTurn, archiveAi } from "../../../lib/archive-ai";

const card = archiveAi.idea;

type Run = { result: string; input: string; code: string; setting: string };
const cols: [keyof Run, string][] = [["result", "Derived result"], ["input", "Raw input"], ["code", "Code version"], ["setting", "Settings"]];
const differs = (runs: Run[], k: keyof Run) => runs.some((r) => r[k] !== runs[0][k]);
const cause = (runs: Run[]) => cols.filter(([k]) => k !== "result" && differs(runs, k)).map(([, name]) => name.toLowerCase());

const usd = (n: number) => `$${n}`;
const lines = [12, 8];
const discount = 3;
const gross = lines.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
const net = gross - discount;
const shop: Run[] = [
  { result: usd(gross), input: "R17", code: "v1", setting: "After discounts" },
  { result: usd(net), input: "R17", code: "v2", setting: "After discounts" },
];

const quiz: [number, number][] = [[2, 3], [1, 2]];
const pct = (x: number) => `${(x * 100).toFixed(1)}%`;
const equalWeight = quiz.reduce((a, [e, p]) => a + e / p, 0) / quiz.length;
const earned = quiz.reduce((a, [e]) => a + e, 0);
const possible = quiz.reduce((a, [, p]) => a + p, 0);
const byPoints = earned / possible;
const grades: Run[] = [
  { result: pct(equalWeight), input: "Q8", code: "v1", setting: "Total points" },
  { result: pct(byPoints), input: "Q8", code: "v2", setting: "Total points" },
];

const drafts = [
  { id: "H4-d1", text: "City council approves new park" },
  { id: "H4-d2", text: "City council approves new riverside park" },
];
const words = (s: string) => s.split(/\s+/).length;
const second = drafts[1];
const check = (limit: number) => `${words(second.text) <= limit ? "Pass" : "Fail"}, ${words(second.text)} words`;
const limits = [words(second.text), words(second.text) - 1];
const headlines: Run[] = limits.map((n) => ({ result: check(n), input: second.id, code: "v1", setting: `Maximum ${n} words` }));
const stores = [
  { id: "kept", label: "Each draft saved under its own ID", held: drafts },
  { id: "over", label: "Second draft saved over the first", held: [second] },
];

if (cause(shop).join() !== "code version" || cause(grades).join() !== "code version" || cause(headlines).join() !== "settings") throw new Error("RawPrimer's records no longer differ in one column each");

const checks = [
  [`The ${usd(net)} total`, `The shop still has R17 and the corrected code. What else does it need to reproduce the corrected ${usd(net)} total?`],
  ["Only the percentage", "A teacher kept only a student's final percentage. Why is that percentage alone insufficient to recalculate the grade under a policy that gives question 2 double weight?"],
  ["Two headline reports", "Two headline reports disagree. Their draft ID and code version match. What would you check next, and what could you recover if the only copy of that draft had been overwritten?"],
];
---

<PrimerChat
  prompt={card.prompt}
  module="Module 2"
  back={`../02-archive/#${aiCardId("idea")}`}
  about="This is a reply an AI assistant gave to Module 2's raw-and-derived study prompt, lightly edited. Send the same prompt yourself and you will get a different reply."
  turn={aiTurn("idea", "Part 2")}
>
  <h2 class="pr-title">Raw data, derived data and replay</h2>
  <p><strong>Raw data</strong> is what arrives at the boundary of your system. Save an exact copy before your processing code reads it. That gives you a record of what you actually received, even if your code misreads a number, drops a field, or rewrites some text. Save each revision as a separate copy when the source can change.</p>
  <p><strong>Derived data</strong> is what your code produces from those inputs. You can delete and rebuild a derived result if you keep the exact raw inputs, the code version, and the settings that produced it. Running the old version can reproduce the old result. Running a corrected version over the same saved inputs is called a <strong>replay</strong>.</p>

  <h2>1. A shop's receipts</h2>
  <p>Receipt <code>R17</code> arrives with two line items, {lines.map(usd).join(" and ")}, and a {usd(discount)} discount. The shop saves the receipt exactly as it arrived. Its daily sales report uses one setting for this example, <code>total = after discounts</code>.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card">
    <p class="pr-label">Two totals and their records</p>
    <table class="pr-table pa-runs">
      <thead><tr>{cols.map(([, name]) => <th scope="col">{name}</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>{shop.map((r) => <tr>{cols.map(([k]) => <td class:list={{ "is-diff": k !== "result" && differs(shop, k) }}>{r[k]}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
    <figcaption>Shaded: the one recorded input that differs between the two records.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>Both rows point to the same receipt and setting. The code changed. Version <code>v1</code> skipped the discount and added {lines.map(usd).join(" + ")}. Version <code>v2</code> computes {lines.map(usd).join(" + ")} − {usd(discount)} = {usd(net)}. Replaying the saved receipt with <code>v2</code> repairs the total. If the shop kept only the {usd(gross)} result and overwrote its copy of <code>R17</code>, that total alone gives it no way to recover the {usd(discount)} discount.</p>

  <h2>2. A teacher's quiz scores</h2>
  <p>The teacher's grading form submits <code>{quiz[0][0]} of {quiz[0][1]}</code> points on question 1 and <code>{quiz[1][0]} of {quiz[1][1]}</code> on question 2. The system saves that submission as <code>Q8</code>. The teacher's setting is <code>percentage = total points earned ÷ total points possible</code>.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card">
    <p class="pr-label">Two grades and their records</p>
    <table class="pr-table pa-runs">
      <thead><tr>{cols.map(([, name]) => <th scope="col">{name}</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>{grades.map((r) => <tr>{cols.map(([k]) => <td class:list={{ "is-diff": k !== "result" && differs(grades, k) }}>{r[k]}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </figure>
  <p>Version <code>v1</code> gave each question equal weight by averaging {quiz.map(([e, p]) => `${e}/${p}`).join(" and ")}. Version <code>v2</code> adds the points first and computes ({quiz.map(([e]) => e).join(" + ")}) ÷ ({quiz.map(([, p]) => p).join(" + ")}) = {pct(byPoints).replace(".0%", "%")}. The records show that the teacher's grading policy stayed the same. Replaying <code>Q8</code> fixes the grade. If the teacher had entered <code>3 of 3</code> on question 1 earlier and changed it before the form was ever submitted, that earlier version is outside the saved record.</p>

  <h2>3. A newsroom's headline drafts</h2>
  <p>An editor submits two drafts. The newsroom saves the exact text of each.</p>
  <ul class="pa-drafts">{drafts.map((d) => <li><code>{d.id}</code> “{d.text}” ({words(d.text)} words)</li>)}</ul>
  <p>A headline checker produces a derived pass or fail result.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card">
    <p class="pr-label">Two checks and their records</p>
    <table class="pr-table pa-runs">
      <thead><tr>{cols.map(([, name]) => <th scope="col">{name}</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>{headlines.map((r) => <tr>{cols.map(([k]) => <td class:list={{ "is-diff": k !== "result" && differs(headlines, k) }}>{r[k]}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </figure>
  <p>Here the raw draft and code are identical. The word limit explains the disagreement. If a checker bug is fixed later, the newsroom can replay both drafts. If it had saved both under the same filename and the second draft overwrote the only copy of the first, replay would have only the second draft to work with.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card pa-store">
    <fieldset class="pr-seg">
      <legend>How the newsroom saved the drafts</legend>
      {stores.map((st, i) => <label><input type="radio" name="pa-raw-drafts" value={st.id} checked={i === 0} /><span>{st.label}</span></label>)}
    </fieldset>
    <div aria-live="polite">
      {stores.map((st) => (
        <ul class="pa-rows" data-store={st.id}>
          {drafts.map((d) => {
            const kept = st.held.includes(d);
            return <li class={kept ? "" : "is-gone"}><span><code>{d.id}</code> {kept ? `“${d.text}”` : "overwritten"}</span><b>{kept ? `replays to ${words(d.text)} words` : "nothing to replay"}</b></li>;
          })}
        </ul>
      ))}
    </div>
    <figcaption>A replay runs only on what was saved. The overwritten draft's words are gone, whatever the checker's code or settings.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <aside class="pr-course" aria-label="Course note">
    <p class="pr-label">Course note</p>
    <p>In Tuesday's story the raw data is the schedule pages Kick saved on Friday, and every table is derived from them. The tidy rule fix is the receipt's case: one code change, replayed over the same saved input. A change to the alias list is the headline's: same pages, same code, a different setting.</p>
    <p>Dunmore's page is like the teacher's unsubmitted entry: the site never answered on Friday, so no copy exists to replay. Its conference results are still on record, because its opponents' saved pages show them. A re-fetch that saves each new page over Friday's copy is the overwritten draft.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>Check your understanding</h2>
  <ol class="pr-checks">{checks.map(([name, q]) => <li><b>{name}</b><span>{q}</span></li>)}</ol>
</PrimerChat>

<style>
  .pa-runs td.is-diff { background: color-mix(in srgb, #d9822b 18%, transparent); font-weight: 600; }
  .pa-runs td:first-child { color: var(--sl-color-white); font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .pa-drafts { margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .pa-store .pr-seg { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
  .pa-store [data-store] { display: none; }
  .pa-store:has([value="kept"]:checked) [data-store="kept"],
  .pa-store:has([value="over"]:checked) [data-store="over"] { display: grid; }
  .pa-rows { gap: 0.35rem; margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .pa-rows li { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; gap: 0.3rem 0.6rem; padding: 0.4rem 0.65rem; border-radius: 6px; background: var(--tl-bg); }
  .pa-rows li b { color: var(--sl-color-white); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .pa-rows li.is-gone { border: 1.5px dashed var(--pr-red); background: transparent; color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-rows li.is-gone b { color: var(--tl-muted); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
